#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a4bbb7d-18d6-5b61-997d-b85518d9f085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{A}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272893} {:answer/id #uuid "947e1701-dda4-539d-abc1-fe98fe7dc964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273680} {:answer/id #uuid "9e50ecf2-3ff8-5c23-8e90-43e354e46ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273752} {:answer/id #uuid "d85bad73-fa5d-5455-afb4-82cf9a4293cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274279} {:answer/id #uuid "fff717bf-fcc8-5e40-9aff-2c2401a6f86d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d85bad73-fa5d-5455-afb4-82cf9a4293cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274279} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5db26d1-9e5a-5da7-a51d-01407ba0e210" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c649e814-a4ed-51c9-ad8d-f8d4317c4c2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-156188" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=A,$ what is the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)}{{e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-x)}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
